  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Missouri has received $392M in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date. The Department of Energy leads buying with $323M in obligations. Award activity shows 9,230 awards so far, and the average award size is $42.5K. Obligations are down 97.0% year over year. Fiscal-year history shows a peak and fall: FY22 obligations were $11.4B, FY23 obligations were $16B, FY24 obligations were $17B, FY25 obligations were $14.5B, and FY26 obligations were $13.1B. Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by obligation in FY2027 to date at $324M. HONEYWELL FEDERAL MANUFACTURING & TECHNOLOGIES, LLC is the largest vendor by obligation at $323M. Other notable buyers include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $22.1M and the Department of Homeland Security at $12.6M. Office Administrative Services recorded $11.4M in obligations. The dataset reflects contract obligations only and the current-year totals are partial because these are fiscal year to date figures.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies dominate federal buying in Missouri?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy is the dominant buyer, obligating $323M in FY2027 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $22.1M and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $12.6M. The Department of Agriculture and the Department of Justice follow with $10.6M and $9.2M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors capture the largest shares of obligations?",
        "body": "Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by obligations at $324M in FY2027 to date. HONEYWELL FEDERAL MANUFACTURING & TECHNOLOGIES, LLC is the largest vendor at $323M. Other vendors with notable obligations include KONIAG BRILLIENT, LLC at $11.4M and ENVIRONMENTAL QUALITY MANAGEMENT, INC at $8.8M."
      }
    ]
  },
